WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.360
While there have been countless male horror&nbsp;
villains, the genre has also been a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.760
space for menacing female leads. Whether they be&nbsp;
young or old, supernatural or mentally deranged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.240
here are some of the scariest female villains&nbsp;
who made us keep our lights on at night.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.200
The best parents always seem to provide&nbsp;
their children with a healthy amount of love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.640
understanding, and stability through good times&nbsp;
and bad. Sadly, that is certainly not the case for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.000
young Carrie White in the 1976 film adaptation&nbsp;
of Stephen King's classic novel "Carrie."

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.480
The movie focuses on the teen's tortured&nbsp;
existence and the endless cruelty she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.640
suffers at her high school, due in part&nbsp;
to her fanatically religious mother.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.760
Margaret White puts the lord before anything else,&nbsp;
especially her daughter, whose adolescent changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.640
only exacerbate the issue. After being informed&nbsp;
of Carrie's incident in the girl's showers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.680
she proceeds to demean Carrie both verbally and&nbsp;
physically. In the most disturbing act of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.520
she locks Carrie in a makeshift prayer closet to&nbsp;
pray away her sin. Her cruelty only intensifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.240
as she attempts to stop Carrie from attending&nbsp;
prom, stating that everyone will laugh at her.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.480
The elder White eventually meets her&nbsp;
end when, after an attempted stabbing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.680
Carrie snaps and assaults her with the help of&nbsp;
her telekinesis. Margaret is often referred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.400
as the true villain of "Carrie," and it's not&nbsp;
an unfair assessment. Carrie, despite her now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.840
infamous actions during the prom night scene,&nbsp;
truly is the victim throughout most of the film.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.880
One of the most popular misconceptions in all&nbsp;
of pop culture is that Jason Voorhees has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.360
been the antagonist in "Friday the 13th."&nbsp;
While Jason is synonymous with the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.000
it was actually his mother, Pamela, holding&nbsp;
the knife in the series' first outing.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.640
The first "Friday the 13th" film, especially&nbsp;
compared to later entries, is noticeably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.000
moodier and slower paced. Additionally, unlike&nbsp;
later entries where Jason was on full display,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.600
the film doesn't reveal the killer's&nbsp;
identity until the final scenes.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.320
Betsy Palmer infamously only took the role because&nbsp;
she needed the money to buy a new car. She even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.880
went as far as to call the script, quote, "a piece&nbsp;
of s---!" and hoped no one would see the film.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.920
Despite those less than enthusiastic impressions,&nbsp;
Palmer doesn't phone in her performance for even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.320
second. Instead of simply being a seemingly&nbsp;
kind, middle-aged woman in a blue sweater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.680
Palmer also manages to bring genuine menace to&nbsp;
the role. She perfectly plays a deranged mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.320
seeking vengeance on the Crystal Lake counselors&nbsp;
after they failed to stop her son from drowning.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.480
"Don't let her live. I won't, Jason. I won't."

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.800
Palmer's performance is still highly regarded&nbsp;
within horror cinema and the catalyst for the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.920
of the series. Her gruesome demise at the film's&nbsp;
climax might have been the end for her character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.720
but it opened door for Jason to become the&nbsp;
iconic killer in all the films that followed.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.960
"Misery" is still considered one of the&nbsp;
best adaptations of Stephen King's work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.240
and it's not hard to see why. Not only is it&nbsp;
impeccably directed by the great Rob Reiner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.680
it also features one of Kathy&nbsp;
Bates' greatest performances.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.560
In the story, writer Paul Sheldon is injured&nbsp;
in a car accident and saved by Annie Wilkes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.760
She initially comes off as a&nbsp;
kind, if not mildly eccentric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.560
woman. Annie introduces herself to Paul as an&nbsp;
extremely enthusiastic fan of his book series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.560
which centers on a character named&nbsp;
Misery. But as the days go on, Annie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.560
enraged at Paul killing off Misery,&nbsp;
reveals her psychotic tendencies.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:27.040
"You dirty bird. How could&nbsp;
you? She can't be dead."

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.680
This leads to Annie both mentally&nbsp;
and physically abusing Paul as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.080
forces him to rewrite the book. Annie's&nbsp;
perfectionist demands and overbearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.880
meddling disrupts Paul's creative process&nbsp;
and the two head towards a deadly finale.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.080
The concept of the psychotic fan is a disturbing&nbsp;
recurring trope that has basis in real life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.440
It's a common occurrence for celebrities&nbsp;
of varying types to be harassed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.320
overly obsessed fans of their work. Bates&nbsp;
portrays that mentality with ample gusto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.040
balancing both manic cheerfulness and&nbsp;
bloodthirsty rage. It was no surprise when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.000
at the subsequent 63rd Academy Awards, Bates&nbsp;
took home an Oscar for Actress in a Leading Role.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.200
Stephen King definitely has a knack for&nbsp;
creating memorable female antagonists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.680
and Rose the Hat is no exception.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.960
"Doctor Sleep," the sequel to "The Shining," picks&nbsp;
up 30 years after Danny Torrance experienced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.160
horrors of the Overlook Hotel. As Danny copes&nbsp;
with both his sobriety and his shining ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.080
a new threat looms. It's revealed&nbsp;
that people with the shining have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.320
become victims to a vampiric cult&nbsp;
known as the True Knot. The cult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.360
led by Rose the Hat, maintains immortality&nbsp;
by consuming the essence of shining users.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.080
The film centers on Danny's fight to protect a&nbsp;
young girl named Abra and her gift from Rose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.640
Despite being the leader of a vicious group&nbsp;
of vampiric monsters, Rose is not a one-note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.400
villain. She legitimately cares for&nbsp;
her clan and is heartbroken when one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.720
of their immortalities finally expires.&nbsp;
However, the film never lets you forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.600
just how cruel and sadistic Rose actually&nbsp;
is at her core. The most horrific example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.760
being a scene when Rose and True Knot kill a&nbsp;
young boy for his essence. She explains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.320
the essence grows stale as people age,&nbsp;
which is why her clan targets children.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.000
Rebecca Ferguson received much praise&nbsp;
for her portrayal of Rose the Hat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.200
injecting the role with equal&nbsp;
parts malice and charisma.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.440
Behind every psychotic serial killing doll there&nbsp;
is an equally psychotic serial killing woman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.560
In the case of Charles Lee&nbsp;
Ray, also known as Chucky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.640
that would be Tiffany Valentine, who was&nbsp;
first introduced in "Bride of Chucky."

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.640
Retroactively created as Chucky's&nbsp;
equally bloodthirsty girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.360
Tiffany succeeds at resurrecting him after&nbsp;
his gruesome demise in "Child's Play 3."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.160
What begins as a joyous, if not bloody reunion&nbsp;
quickly goes south as Chucky reveals his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.320
dismissive feelings towards Tiffany.&nbsp;
This results in serious relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.360
issues as Chucky electrocutes Tiffany in&nbsp;
her own bathtub. Then, as a spiteful act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.240
he utilizes the series' recurring voodoo&nbsp;
curse to trap Tiffany's soul in another doll.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.680
The plastic duo then tricks a young couple, Jesse&nbsp;
and Jade, into transporting them to New Jersey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.600
Along the way, Chucky and Tiffany spill&nbsp;
a fair amount of blood and rekindle their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.640
undoubtedly twisted but endearing romance. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.520
the honeymoon doesn't last long and Chucky starts&nbsp;
to severely criticize Tiffany's domestic skills.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.560
"What would Martha Stewart say?”

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.000
“F--- Martha Stewart!"

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.600
Tiffany was so well received&nbsp;
by "Child's Play" fans that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.080
she was brought back for three additional sequels.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.480
Beware the stare of Mary&nbsp;
Shaw. She had no children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.680
only dolls. And if you see her in your&nbsp;
dreams, be sure you never, ever scream.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.080
Whether it's Billy from "Saw" or Annabelle&nbsp;
from "The Conjuring," director James Wan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.240
clearly has a fascination with dolls&nbsp;
and puppets. But the precedent for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.120
recurring trend in his work was set back in&nbsp;
his third theatrical release "Dead Silence."

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.280
The film follows Jamie Ashen, who, after the&nbsp;
death of his wife, returns to his hometown for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.000
answers — answers concerning a mysterious&nbsp;
ventriloquist doll and the infamous local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.880
legend about a woman known as Mary Shaw. She&nbsp;
was a renowned ventriloquist who didn't take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.760
kindly to a rude child interrupting her act&nbsp;
and saying that he could see her lips move.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.520
"Who said that?"

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.720
"I did. I can see it. Her lips are moving"

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.280
When the child mysteriously goes missing, the&nbsp;
townspeople hunt Mary down and kill her. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.080
Mary's final wish was, fittingly, to be&nbsp;
turned into a dummy and buried with her dolls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.200
Now in a new ghostly form, complete with&nbsp;
soulless eyes and ventriloquist doll mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.680
she seeks vengeance on Jamie's bloodline.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.400
Throughout Mary's long reign of terror, she&nbsp;
leaves a disgusting trademark on her victims.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.800
She rips their tongues out and gives them&nbsp;
horrific puppet-like scars on their faces.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.840
"Now, who's the dummy?"

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.200
"The Loved Ones" is often regarded as an&nbsp;
underrated gem from the past 15 years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.040
horror cinema. The film centers around&nbsp;
a high schooler named Brent who ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.840
in the crosshairs of his demented classmate,&nbsp;
Lola. After declining her offer to go to prom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.720
Brent, still coping with his father's death,&nbsp;
is kidnapped by her. He's then forced into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.920
disturbing mock prom by Lola and her father&nbsp;
while he's mentally and physically tortured.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.040
Lola, played by Robin McLeavy, completely steals&nbsp;
the show. Her performance rides the fine line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:18.640
between disturbing and campy, but never once does&nbsp;
McLeavy go too over the top. Lola at no point in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.800
the film sees anything wrong with what she's&nbsp;
doing to Brent. She just views the situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.880
in her demented mind, as the perfect&nbsp;
prom night with the cutest guy in school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.120
This delusion is aided by her equally&nbsp;
insane and uncomfortably close father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.760
who helps her with this sickening act. The&nbsp;
scale of Lola and her father's crimes only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.680
increases throughout the film, which&nbsp;
takes several bizarre twists and turns.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.640
The true terror of Lola is that throughout the&nbsp;
entire movie, she could do anything at any moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.800
She goes from playfully trying to feed Brent&nbsp;
in one scene to threatening to horrifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.840
mutilate him in the next. It's a performance&nbsp;
and film that deserves far more attention.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.280
"Orphan" has received a lot of praise and&nbsp;
criticism since it was first released in 2009.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.040
However, the one thing all viewers can&nbsp;
agree on is that the character of Esther,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.000
played by Isabelle Fuhrman, is completely&nbsp;
shocking, unique, and unforgettable.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.080
At the beginning of the film, Kate and John&nbsp;
Coleman hope to heal their strained marriage by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.800
adopting a third child. They're both immediately&nbsp;
drawn to a young Russian girl named Esther and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.600
decide to adopt her. Things almost immediately&nbsp;
turn sinister as Esther becomes increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.800
manipulative and injures a school bully. As her&nbsp;
transgressions evolve, she begins to reveal her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.400
true sadistic nature. Her evil goal is to remove&nbsp;
her adopted mother entirely from the picture.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.640
Kate eventually discovers that Esther has no&nbsp;
documented records and a lot of people in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.320
history have ended up six feet under. All of that&nbsp;
information, however, is upstaged by the big twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:48.080
that Esther has a rare hormone condition and is&nbsp;
actually a mentally deranged 33-year-old woman.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.800
"Stop talking to me like I'm a child."

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.840
After the film's original release, Fuhrman&nbsp;
received accolades for her disturbing portrayal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:00.240
of Esther. Her performance was so haunting that&nbsp;
Fuhrman, with the help of some special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.840
will reprise the role in an&nbsp;
upcoming prequel to the film.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.640
Jordan Peele's "Us" has been hailed as one of&nbsp;
the most thoughtful and original horror films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.920
in recent years. It's the story of the Wilson&nbsp;
family and a vacation that goes horribly wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.720
The mother, Adelaide, portrayed by Lupita&nbsp;
Nyong'o, is on edge almost immediately due a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.880
childhood trauma that occurred in the area. When&nbsp;
she was walking through a hall of mirrors at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.920
local carnival, she was attacked by a doppelganger&nbsp;
of herself called a "tether." Many years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.880
that memory has taken on a dangerous new reality.&nbsp;
A group of tethers, which seem to be a version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.720
of her entire family, start to wreak havoc at&nbsp;
the command of their mother, a woman named Red.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.520
A dangerous duplicate of Adelaide, Red quickly&nbsp;
makes her intentions clear as she regales the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.640
terrified Wilsons with a horrific monologue.&nbsp;
Whenever on screen, Red speaks in a haunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:54.000
and almost withered voice, which only serves to&nbsp;
heighten her menace. Add to that her thousand-yard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.760
stare, physical prowess, and unstoppable drive —&nbsp;
and it's impossible to not be frightened by her.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.120
It's eventually revealed that&nbsp;
Adelaide herself is the actual tether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.360
having snatched and swapped places with her&nbsp;
double during their childhood encounter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:10.560
Nyong'o's double performance&nbsp;
truly is an impressive feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.320
and it's still disappointing to many fans&nbsp;
that she wasn't nominated for an Oscar.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.360
One of the best characters in Rob&nbsp;
Zombie's "Firefly Trilogy" is Baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.680
played by Sherri Moon Zombie, a member of&nbsp;
the disturbing backwoods serial killer cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.480
gang known as The Fireflies. Flanked by&nbsp;
the likes of Otis and Captain Spaulding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.960
it's quite impressive that Baby stands out as&nbsp;
the craziest. First introduced in "House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.560
1000 Corpses," Baby is a Hollywood-obsessed and&nbsp;
overly bubbly serial killer, carrying out all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.840
of her family's heinous deeds with a sense of&nbsp;
glee and a smile on her face. Moon Zombie has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:50.000
clear appreciation and understanding of the role,&nbsp;
having played it three times from 2003 to 2019.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.760
Over the course of the following films,&nbsp;
"The Devil's Rejects" and "3 From Hell,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.800
Baby's insanity would only increase.&nbsp;
Despite her bloodthirsty and cruel nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:03.120
Baby still maintains a strangely endearing&nbsp;
charm, a recurring theme in Rob Zombie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.920
work. This is best illustrated by a delightful&nbsp;
scene where Baby and Spaulding pester Otis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:12.480
into stopping for ice cream. Baby, despite&nbsp;
being a bloodcrazed psychopath, does seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.440
have a lot of affection and appreciation&nbsp;
for her entire criminally insane family.

